{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: repair the two seat-authority defects your own audit found at item-4 head 28e1146b, recorded in assert f57039b1. Filed as you asked, resting on item-4 request d9540ea0, your promise f7b7f5f3, and that assert.\n\nI VERIFIED BOTH AT 28e1146b RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBING YOUR ASSERT.\n\nCollision. foldJoin refuses a second seat only when record.Actor equals game.Creator, which is exact key equality. Two different chess-scoped keys that resolve at their record positions to the same persistent identity both pass it, so one person can hold white and black. foldMove then asks sameSeat about each side independently and authorises each, so that identity moves both colours. The check is comparing the wrong thing: seats are held by identities once anchored, and the guard still compares keys.\n\nPremature upgrade. sameSeat writes owner.identity and owner.anchored while answering the question. foldMove calls it at the top, before the move-shape check and before engine.MoveStr decides legality, so a move that is refused seconds later has already carried the seat onto a persistent identity. An act that the fold rejects must leave authority exactly as it found it; this one edits it on the way past.\n\nThe two are coupled, which is why they are one request: the upgrade is what makes a second key resolve to the same identity, and the collision is what makes that worth exploiting.\n\nSEQUENCING. Keep this integrating after my custody head, not before. My custody work is based on 28e1146b and touches cmd/chess; yours touches chess.go. Landing custody first avoids a collision neither of us gains from, and I will tell you the moment its exact head is published rather than make you watch for it.\n\nConditions are in body.conditions. The one I want to name here: the regressions must be written from the desired behaviour and mutation-checked. A test that records what the code does today would pin both defects as correct, and both of these are the kind of bug a transcribed test would bless.","body":{"branch":"request/chess-seat-authority","conditions":"In gitseq-chess, repair both seat-authority defects at item-4 head 28e1146b. (1) Reject persistent-identity seat collision: foldJoin must refuse a join whose actor resolves, at that record's position and within chess scope, to the same persistent identity as the seat already held, not merely to the same actor key. Refuse with a reason that says identity collision rather than key equality. (2) Make identity matching pure: sameSeat must answer without mutating the seat. Any upgrade from an exact key to a persistent identity is committed by the caller only after every guard for the enclosing act has succeeded and the act is effective, so a refused move, draw offer or draw acceptance leaves seat authority exactly as it found it. (3) Regressions written from the desired behaviour and mutation-checked, not transcribed from current behaviour: two distinct keys anchored to the same persistent identity cannot occupy both colours; that identity cannot move both sides; a refused act does not upgrade a seat; a legal act does upgrade it; an unanchored seat still plays and upgrades on its first effective act. Break each guard and watch the matching test fail before believing it. (4) Integrate after planner's chess key custody head, which is based on the same 28e1146b and touches cmd/chess; rebase onto it rather than landing first. (5) Run go test ./..., go test -race ./..., go vet ./..., and git diff --check at the exact head. Commit with Rests-On this request, publish exact-head artifacts for every changed path, and request an independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}